  "id": "derived-asp-2d6.2",
  "original_statement": "In one sentence, what is the user-facing outcome you want? A Rust consumer gets the whole Aspergillus rule set, not a subset: every ASP rule id that exists for Python has a blocking Rust check with fixtures, or docs/design.md states why the rule does not apply to Rust.\nWho is it for? Who uses this and in what situation? Maintainers of Rust repositories adopting Aspergillus (first consumer lobaria).\nHow does a person USE the finished thing \u2014 what do they SEE and DO? Describe the observable behavior, i.e. how you would demo it. docs/design.md's Rust column names an enforcing check for each of ASP302, ASP303, ASP304, ASP401, ASP402, ASP405, ASP406, ASP407, ASP408, ASP409, ASP410, ASP411, ASP412, ASP413, ASP414, ASP415, ASP416 and ASP417, or a one-line reason it does not apply to Rust (for example a rule the Rust type system already enforces, naming the language feature). Each enforced rule runs from the gate binary or a clippy lint the gate forces at deny, prints `path:line: ASPnnn`, blocks, and has a committed violating and satisfying fixture pair. Rules with a direct Rust analogue are enforced, for example: ASP303 an error swallowed into a success value (`.ok()` discarding an Err, `let _ =` on a Result, `unwrap_or_default` on a Result); ASP304 a failure that drops its evidence (`map_err(|_| ..)` discarding the source error); ASP411 match arms with identical bodies; ASP413 a wildcard arm over an enum; ASP414 dispatch on string literals that shadow an enum's variants; ASP416 a function over its input that returns bool or Result<(), E> instead of the parsed type. ASP501 also finds duplicated statement sequences inside otherwise different functions, as the Python check-duplicates CLI does (whole-body + sliding-window + type-1). ASP502 also finds a duplicated type under a different name or with reordered fields (same field names and types). ASP203 has a fixture pair.\nWhat does 'done' look like in plain language? State the acceptance you could check without reading any code. docs/design.md Rust column: no entry reads 'Python only', 'Manual', 'planned' or 'not yet ported' for any ASP id listed above. For every enforced rule, running the gate over its violating fixture prints that rule id and exits 1, and over its satisfying fixture prints nothing for it and exits 0. ASP502 over a two-crate probe with `struct Lamports(pub u64)` and `struct Balance(pub u64)` reports both; with `struct P{a:i32,b:i32}` and `struct P{b:i32,a:i32}` reports both. ASP501 over a probe with a 6-statement block copied into two otherwise different functions reports both. cargo test, cargo clippy -D warnings and cargo fmt --check pass in rust/.\nWhat are the non-goals / constraints? What must this NOT do? Not ASP202/204/205/206 (a sibling pebble fixes those). No report-only rule: every enforced rule blocks. Not a Python or TypeScript change. ASP415 (exophial reducer reachability) may be stated as not applicable if it is exophial-specific.\nAny examples or references? (links, prior art, a similar thing you like) python/src/aspergillus/rules/level3.py (ASP302-304), rules/catalog/*.py (ASP401-417), python/src/aspergillus/duplicates.py and __main__.py check-duplicates (fragment + type-1 passes). Verification 2026-09-23 (cloud agents VerifyASP202, VerifyASP205_206, VerifySingleFileRules, VerifyDupRules, VerifyLobariaGate; aspergillus integrated 019f8b9, lobaria integrated d0269c7): ASP501 is whole-body only and misses copied statement spines; ASP502 misses a renamed duplicate and a field reorder; ASP203 has no fixture pair.\nWhat priority is this? (P0\u2013P4) and why now? P1\n\nDecomposed child: Make the Rust ASP501 check find duplicated statement sequences inside otherwise different functions, as the Python check-duplicates CLI does (whole-body + sliding-window + type-1). The audience is maintainers of Rust repositories that adopt Aspergillus. The first consumer is lobaria.\n\nA verification on 2026-09-23 (cloud agent VerifyDupRules; aspergillus integrated 019f8b9, lobaria integrated d0269c7) found that ASP501 is whole-body only and misses copied statement spines.\n\nAcceptance: ASP501 over a probe with a 6-statement block copied into two otherwise different functions reports both. The check runs from the gate binary, or from a clippy lint that the gate forces at deny. The check prints `path:line: ASP501`. The check blocks. The check has a committed violating fixture and a committed satisfying fixture. Running the gate over the violating fixture prints ASP501 and exits 1. Running the gate over the satisfying fixture prints nothing for ASP501 and exits 0.\n\nNon-goals: no report-only behavior; the check blocks. This is not a Python or TypeScript change.\n\ncargo test, cargo clippy -D warnings and cargo fmt --check pass in rust/.\n\nPrior art: python/src/aspergillus/duplicates.py and python/src/aspergillus/__main__.py check-duplicates (fragment + type-1 passes).\n\nPriority P1.",
  "restatement": "Aspergillus ships a Rust gate that reports rule findings over a Cargo workspace. Its ASP501 duplicate check today compares only whole function bodies. A verification on 2026-09-23 (cloud agent VerifyDupRules; aspergillus integrated 019f8b9, lobaria integrated d0269c7) confirmed this, so the Rust check misses a copied statement spine that sits inside two functions that differ elsewhere. The Python check-duplicates command already finds that case. It runs three passes: a whole-body pass, a sliding-window pass over statement sequences, and a type-1 exact-clone pass. This request makes the Rust ASP501 check do the same three passes. The audience is the maintainers of Rust repositories that adopt Aspergillus. The first consumer is lobaria. The check must find a 6-statement block that a person copied into two otherwise different functions, and must report both functions. The check must reach the maintainer through the gate binary, or through a clippy lint that the gate forces at deny. Each finding prints in the existing format, `path:line: ASP501`. The check blocks. It is never report-only. The repository carries a violating fixture and a satisfying fixture for ASP501. The gate over the violating fixture prints ASP501 and exits 1. The gate over the satisfying fixture prints nothing for ASP501 and exits 0. The Rust toolchain stays clean: cargo test, cargo clippy -D warnings and cargo fmt --check all pass in rust/. The request changes no Python code and no TypeScript code.",
